  "cause": "**The Vice-President resigned to parliament on Thursday, and the resignation settles the politics without answering the question that caused it.** **Gibran Rakabuming Raka** tendered his resignation in person; **Prabowo Subianto** accepted it standing beside him *\"with a heavy heart,\"* praising his *\"chivalrous attitude, a strong sense of responsibility, and the qualities of a statesman,\"* and saying the decision was *\"for the benefit of the nation, the state, and the people of Indonesia.\"* On the substance the president offered a cultural relativism rather than a finding: *\"He certainly believes he completed his education through a proper and structured process. However, we must understand that educational systems abroad differ from those in Indonesia.\"* The **Supreme Court** had already found otherwise — *\"No evidence was found to show Gibran had a foreign diploma or certificate that could serve as the basis for equivalency with a senior high school or equivalent qualification\"* — following a **Constitutional Court** ruling in October to the same effect, against a law requiring a vice-president to have completed senior secondary school or its equivalent. He had studied in Singapore and Australia and relied on an Indonesian equivalence certificate. More than **100** lawmakers had backed the initiative that could have led to impeachment — **120** from eight parties as of Wednesday. The upper house now has **60 days** to fill the vacancy from two candidates nominated by the president. **The alliance that produced him is unwinding in public while both principals deny it.** Gibran's elevation in 2024 was the price of the **Jokowi**–**Prabowo** pact, and it required a Constitutional Court ruling exempting him from the age-40 minimum when he was **36**. Since then Prabowo has shown little interest in Jokowi's legacy project at **Nusantara**, redirecting resources to free school meals; he has reshuffled Jokowi loyalists including finance minister **Sri Mulyani Indrawati**, and sacked the former national police chief **Listyo Sigit Prabowo**, widely read as a Jokowi ally, a day after his own appointment. On Monday three cabinet members resigned from the party led by **Kaesang Pangarep**, Jokowi's youngest son, to focus on their government duties. On Thursday Jokowi said he still supports Prabowo, called his son's resignation *\"political dynamics,\"* and said he had told him *\"the position was temporary.\"* **And the government's other number is still frozen at July.** Official burned-area data has not been updated since **July**, when **202,000 hectares** had burned; **Auriga** counts more than **637,000** hectares for January–August and **Nusantara Atlas** puts it near **900,000**. The haze closed schools in Indonesia, East Timor and Malaysian states; nearly **2,000** flights were affected between 11 August and 12 October. The fires are the country's worst in a decade, the free school meals programme is struggling for funding, the stock market is in crisis amid allegations of widespread market manipulation, and students and activists are demonstrating over the environmental crisis and corruption. **SMRC** has the president at **44.5%**, down from **81.2%** a year ago — the lowest of any Indonesian president in twenty years of polling.",
  "bullets": [
   "**A resignation is a settlement that leaves the precedent standing** — the office is vacated, and the upper house fills it within 60 days from two candidates named by the president who was his running mate; the direct effect is that the constitutional question is closed without a finding; the compound consequence is that the entry requirement has now been tested once, found unenforced for nineteen months, and repaired by removal rather than by verification — so the next candidate with an inconvenient transcript faces the same route, and the remedy is now a resignation instead of a rule",
   "**The verification that mattered was never performed, and it was never anybody's job** — a Supreme Court finding of *no evidence* after inauguration, a Constitutional Court ruling in October, an equivalence certificate the ministry was ordered to withdraw; the direct effect is that the illegitimacy was discoverable years before the ballot; the compound consequence is that the fix is procedural and cheap — published candidate-document verification before nomination papers are accepted — and that its absence is the actual cause of this week, not the man who benefited from it",
   "**\"The position was temporary\" is a sentence a family says about an office it is already leaving, and it tells you what the family thinks a vice-presidency was for** — Jokowi on his son's tenure, alongside three cabinet resignations from Kaesang's party on Monday and the removal of a police chief read as a Jokowi ally; the direct effect is that the alliance's denial outlives its offices; the compound consequence is that a coalition which loses a vice-president, a police chief and three party members in a fortnight is a coalition whose remaining members are pricing exits — and the legislation that stalls first is the legislation that costs money, which is the fire-and-haze budget and the school-meals funding",
   "**A frozen hectare series next to a resigning vice-president is one government's answer to two questions, and the answer is the same** — 202,000 hectares official since July against 637,000 and 900,000 from independent monitors, in the worst fire season in a decade; the direct effect is that the public cannot know the scale of what it is breathing; the compound consequence is that the monitors now own the number, so the state's remaining instrument is denial — and denial is not available for the fires or for the certificate, because both have been counted by someone else",
   "**A 44.5% approval rating is not a cause, it is a measurement of one, and the measurement is inside the window where coalitions reprice** — down from 81.2%, with demonstrations, a market in crisis and a party split inside a fortnight; the direct effect is that every remaining partner calculates its position against the 2029 election rather than the current law; the compound consequence is that a weakened executive cannot enforce a concession moratorium against the interests that fund it — so the peat keeps burning in the permits it cannot revoke, and the vice-presidency is filled by the arithmetic of survival rather than by any standard"
  ],
  "verse": "Act only in the name of the truth and liberate yourselves from your belief (assumptions) in gods and tin gods… and from belief (assumptions) in those who raise themselves up amongst people of your kind (human beings) as gods or as their envoys or appointees (substitutes); act (live) out of yourselves in equitableness (fairness), righteousness (conscientiousness), as fair ones (responsible ones)… and the false prophets who lie that they are bringing you the truth, but are however lying to you and spreading delusional teachings…",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 9, Verse 105",
  "hinge": "§9:105 makes truth a matter of *acting* in its name, not of believing one is acting in it — and it names the mechanism precisely: those who *lie that they are bringing you the truth*. A president saying a man *believes* he completed his education is a statement about belief offered where a statement about records belongs; a court had already looked for the record and found none. Two publications, both inside the 60 days, close this: a candidate-eligibility verification standard, applied before nomination papers are accepted and published in full, so the question is settled at the ballot-printing stage rather than after nineteen months in office; and a monthly burned-area release with the concession names attached, broken down by province, restoring the series the ministry stopped in July. The first answers the vice-president. The second answers the air. Neither requires a new law, and a government that resumes counting is a government that can still be argued with — which is the only kind that survives an approval rating of 44.5%."
